{"version": "v1_reader", "dice_core": {"dice": "1d100", "compare": "roll_under", "summary": "Call of Cthulhu 7e uses a percentile core. The player rolls 1d100 and compares the result to the relevant skill or characteristic. If the roll is equal to or below the target, the attempt succeeds; if it is above, it fails. The Keeper sets difficulty by reducing the target to half for Hard or to one-fifth for Extreme. In opposed situations, each side rolls against its own target/difficulty and the higher level of success wins; on a tie, the defender wins. Fumbles are special catastrophic failures: for targets of 50 or more, only a 100 fumbles; for targets below 50, 96-100 fumbles. Sanity uses the same roll-under percentile structure, but failure causes the listed Sanity loss for the encounter, and a SAN fumble means the maximum loss. Damage is handled separately from the check, usually by subtracting rolled damage from hit points after armor.", "direction": "roll_under", "compare_to": "target skill/characteristic or opposed difficulty", "target_face": null, "success_rule": "Roll 1d100 equal to or under the target number to succeed. If the check is opposed, the side with the higher level of success wins; if both achieve the same level, the defender wins. If the roll is a fumble, it is an automatic failure and may worsen results in some subsystems.", "success_bands": [{"id": "regular", "rank": 1, "test": {"kind": "roll_under_or_equal"}, "label": "Success"}, {"id": "hard", "rank": 2, "test": {"kind": "roll_under_fraction", "numerator": 1, "denominator": 2}, "label": "Hard Success"}, {"id": "extreme", "rank": 3, "test": {"kind": "roll_under_fraction", "numerator": 1, "denominator": 5}, "label": "Extreme Success"}, {"id": "fumble", "rank": 0, "test": {"max": 100, "min": 96, "kind": "in_range"}, "label": "Fumble", "unless": {"target_gte": 50}}], "target_number": null, "success_threshold": null}, "kernel_id": "call_of_cthulhu_7e.rule_kernel.v1", "play_loop": {"gm_procedures": "Keeper-facing play is about setting mood, framing investigations, and pushing pressure through information, fear, and consequences. Before play, choose the tone with the group (serious, jocular, pulpier, etc.), prepare a distraction-free space, low light, and atmospheric audio if desired, and remember the game’s default mode is horror with some action—not combat-first. In scene framing, present a clear situation and likely leads, then ask what the investigators do; use skill rolls when an action’s outcome is uncertain, and don’t make players roll for things that are simply routine or can be revealed by careful play. The example play shows the Keeper adjudicating with one or two short checks, then immediately delivering concrete clues on success and moving the action onward. Keep clues flowing: if investigators split up, be clear about who knows what, and when they miss or misread a clue, make the consequence a new complication rather than a dead end. The campaign loop is investigative: investigators explore, uncover clues, face sanity-shaking revelations, gain skills/knowledge over time, and either survive, retire, or die. Between sessions/adventures, award Sanity recovery and other advancement as appropriate, and use downtime for treatment, research, and recovery. Minimal one-session prep: know the scenario’s central mystery, each required clue/scene, likely NPC reactions, the horror reveal, and a few ways the investigators can move from one lead to the next if they get stuck.", "subsystem_map": "Investigation and clue-finding: central. Sanity and mental breakdown: central. Occult tomes/literary lore and spell study: central. Magic/spells: one_of_several. Chases: minor to one_of_several depending on scenario, but not the game’s core. Combat: minor to one_of_several, generally secondary to investigation and horror. Character advancement/downtime treatment: one_of_several."}, "ruleset_id": "call_of_cthulhu_7e", "check_model": {"compare": "roll_under", "summary": "Call of Cthulhu 7e uses a percentile core. The player rolls 1d100 and compares the result to the relevant skill or characteristic. If the roll is equal to or below the target, the attempt succeeds; if it is above, it fails. The Keeper sets difficulty by reducing the target to half for Hard or to one-fifth for Extreme. In opposed situations, each side rolls against its own target/difficulty and the higher level of success wins; on a tie, the defender wins. Fumbles are special catastrophic failures: for targets of 50 or more, only a 100 fumbles; for targets below 50, 96-100 fumbles. Sanity uses the same roll-under percentile structure, but failure causes the listed Sanity loss for the encounter, and a SAN fumble means the maximum loss. Damage is handled separately from the check, usually by subtracting rolled damage from hit points after armor.", "direction": "roll_under", "compare_to": "target skill/characteristic or opposed difficulty", "target_face": null, "success_rule": "Roll 1d100 equal to or under the target number to succeed. If the check is opposed, the side with the higher level of success wins; if both achieve the same level, the defender wins. If the roll is a fumble, it is an automatic failure and may worsen results in some subsystems.", "target_number": null, "success_threshold": null}, "source_refs": [{"note": null, "page": 1, "char_end": null, "anchor_id": "call_of_cthulhu_keeper_rulebook_40th_anniversary_sandy_petersen.p0001", "source_id": "call_of_cthulhu_keeper_rulebook_40th_anniversary_sandy_petersen", "text_hash": "sha256:4e1ed33026f7d1fd1893148a6895450264754f824c5d812ba9bf9d69d1de67bc", "char_start": null, "section_path": []}], "game_identity": {"summary": "Call of Cthulhu 7th Edition is a Lovecraftian investigative horror RPG: fragile investigators, cosmic dread, occult study, and long-term survival/retirement rather than tactical heroics."}, "contest_models": [], "object_schemas": [{"kind": "tome", "examples": [{"name": "Confessions of the Mad Monk", "slots": {"sanity_loss": {"expr": "2D6"}, "mythos_rating": 27, "study_time_weeks": 29, "suggested_spells": "Call Down the Blasphemies of the Oceans (Contact Star-Spawn of Cthulhu), Sign of the Master (Elder Sign), Warding.", "language_or_version": "Latin, by Clithanus, 400 (1674)", "cthulhu_mythos_gain_full": 6, "cthulhu_mythos_gain_initial": 3}, "source_pages": "239"}, {"name": "De Vermiis Mysteriis", "slots": {"sanity_loss": {"expr": "2D6"}, "mythos_rating": 36, "study_time_weeks": 48, "suggested_spells": "Contact Deity/Yig, Create Scrying Window, Create Zombie, Invoke Demon (Summon/Bind Byakhee), Invoke Child of the Goat (Summon/Bind Dark Young), Invoke Invisible Servant (Summon/Bind Star Vampire), Mind Transfer, Voorish Sign.", "language_or_version": "Latin, by Ludwig Prinn, 1542", "cthulhu_mythos_gain_full": 8, "cthulhu_mythos_gain_initial": 4}, "source_pages": "240"}], "validation": {"provisional": 0, "slot_values_checked": 14}, "category_id": "mythos_tomes", "schema_slots": [{"hook": "automatic sanity loss suffered on reading/studying the tome; applies to the sanity resource track", "slot": "sanity_loss", "type": "dice"}, {"hook": "first Cthulhu Mythos skill gain from initial study of the tome", "slot": "cthulhu_mythos_gain_initial", "type": "number"}, {"hook": "maximum/total Cthulhu Mythos skill gain obtainable from fully studying the tome", "slot": "cthulhu_mythos_gain_full", "type": "number"}, {"hook": "caps/benchmarks the tome's Mythos potency for study and Keeper comparison", "slot": "mythos_rating", "type": "number"}, {"hook": "weeks required to study the tome before gaining its benefits", "slot": "study_time_weeks", "type": "number"}, {"hook": "spells learnable from the tome; links tome entries to grimoire spell entries", "slot": "suggested_spells", "type": "text"}, {"hook": "identifies which textual tradition/translation is being used for availability and comprehension context", "slot": "language_or_version", "type": "text"}], "provisional_slots": []}, {"kind": "spell", "examples": [{"name": "Mirror of Tarkhun Atep", "slots": {"range": "anywhere on Earth", "effect": "the caster is able to project his or her image onto the surface of a mirror, or mirroring surface, that is being gazed into by the caster’s intended victim.", "cost_pow": null, "cost_sanity": {"expr": "1"}, "opposed_pow": null, "casting_time": "half a day", "cost_magic_points": 5, "damage_or_protection": null}, "source_pages": "273"}, {"name": "Song of Hastur", "slots": {"range": "The target must be visible to the caster.", "effect": "A hideous wailing ululation by the caster causes the skin and flesh of the chosen victim to bubble and fester into pestilent blobs. Successfully cast, the spell causes the target 1D6 hit points damage per round the spell is active. After every two rounds, scarring reduces the victim’s APP by 3D10. After every four rounds, internal ruptures lower the victim’s CON by 3D10.", "cost_pow": null, "cost_sanity": {"expr": "1D4"}, "opposed_pow": null, "casting_time": "3 rounds before it begins to take effect", "cost_magic_points": null, "damage_or_protection": {"expr": "1D6"}}, "source_pages": "275"}], "validation": {"provisional": 5, "slot_values_checked": 16}, "category_id": "spells", "schema_slots": [{"hook": "spent from the caster's magic-point cost noted in spell entries; mechanically a resource cost even though not in provided track list", "slot": "cost_magic_points", "type": "number"}, {"hook": "sanity loss paid when casting; applies to the sanity resource track", "slot": "cost_sanity", "type": "dice"}, {"hook": "permanent or temporary POW expenditure when specified by the spell", "slot": "cost_pow", "type": "number"}, {"hook": "determines when the spell resolves in combat/time structure", "slot": "casting_time", "type": "text"}, {"hook": "uses the spell-range model described as touch / 100 yards / sight or specific text in the entry", "slot": "range", "type": "range_ref"}, {"hook": "whether the spell resolves via oppose